  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA southwest to zonal flow patterns sets up from Tuesday through the \nremainder of the forecast, with multiple shortwaves passing through \nat various times in the week. This will keep the forecast semi-\nactive, with at least low chances for rain in Iowa each day \nWednesday through Sunday. Wednesday night into early Thursday will \nbe our next chance for widespread rain, primarily overnight. This \nprecipitation may also be accompanied by some marginal instability \n(~500 to 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE), as well as a healthy 30 to 40 kt low \nlevel jet overnight. It's of course far too early to get deep into \nspecifics, but if that scenario plays out, a few stronger storms \ncould be possible in that Wednesday/Thursday timeframe, likely \nleaning toward elevated, nocturnal storms for our area. \n\nBeyond the mid-week system, there is actually pretty good agreement \namong deterministic and ensemble guidance for another wave and \nhealthy plume of moisture to lift up into Iowa Friday through \nSaturday, bringing the potential for more widespread rain and \nthunderstorms. Both 23.13z NBM probabilities and 23.12z LREF (GEFS, \nEPS, and GEPS driven) probabilities show a widespread 40 to 50%\nchance for greater than 0.01\" of 6 hr rainfall throughout \nFriday into Friday evening, increasing to a 50 to 70% \nprobability for the same on Saturday. The LREF cluster analysis \nis also quite impressive, with 3 of the 4 cluster solutions \nindicating a 50% or greater chance for rain on Saturday, and the\nremaining solution depicting stronger ridging and a delayed \narrival of the rainfall into Saturday night or Sunday. All this \nto say, the current guidance is pointing toward a good chance \nfor rain falling over the area next weekend. However, keep in \nmind that while ensemble models are more dispersive, they do \nstill change as the pattern does. We will have to keep an eye on\nhow things trend in the coming days. Regardless, the current \nforecast looks relatively active for Iowa through the long term.",
